**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` still serves every read and write. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` copies those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously and in commit order, while `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night over about 11 nights. Nobody reads orders-v2 yet, so mistakes are invisible to customers. Phase 1 passes when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate is under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 is under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a single full-table checksum matches, which is the only check that covers every old order.

**Cutover (phase 2).** The flag `orders.read_from_v2` moves reads to orders-v2 one step a day (1%, 10%, 50%, then 100%) while writes still go to both stores. Reads then stay at 100% for a 48-hour soak. After that, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off, the relay is scaled to zero, and legacy stays read-only for 30 days before it is decommissioned under `CHG-4471`. Until the write stop, rolling back just means setting the read flag back to 0. After it there is no way back, because nothing syncs orders-v2 to legacy. That is why the write stop needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director.

The schedule has very little slack. The write stop is planned for 2026-11-30, the day before the code freeze. If it slips, dual-write keeps running through the freeze until January.

**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` remains the source of truth for every read and write. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` (6 replicas, idempotent on `(order_id, version)`) applies those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously. Meanwhile `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders overnight. No one reads orders-v2 yet, so errors there can't reach customers. Hourly `order-diff` checks and alerts `ORD-310`, `ORD-311` and `ORD-315` watch the copy.

The phase ends only when four criteria are met:
- the backfill is complete;
- the mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds over those same days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches, since the hourly checker only samples old orders.

**Cutover (phase 2).** The `orders.read_from_v2` flag moves reads to orders-v2 at 1%, 10%, 50% and then 100%, one step a day, while writes still go to both stores. Reads then stay at 100% for a 48-hour soak. Until that point, rollback means setting the flag back to 0, and it loses nothing because legacy has received every write.

After the soak, with sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off and `outbox-relay` is scaled to zero. This step is one-way: nothing syncs from orders-v2 back to legacy, so falling back afterward would lose every order written since. Legacy then stays read-only for 30 days and is decommissioned under `CHG-4471`. The cutover counts as done after 7 days with no `ORD-4xx` incident and order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

**Schedule.** The timeline has no slack. The four read-ramp steps take exactly 2026-11-24 to 11-27 and the soak runs to 11-29, so holding any step for even one day pushes the write stop past 2026-11-30. The code freeze then delays it to January, and dual-write keeps running through the freeze at its current cost.
